module Incomplete.Gu07 where

{- INFERENCE GIVES UP WHERE CHECKING SUCCEEDS -- the library author's version,
   2 of 3: the caller that does not.

   THE SCENARIO.  Gu06's `withLabel` helper, used once, for exactly the reason
   it was written: SalesByRegion's rule that a direct sale is labelled with the
   product name and a partner sale with "product via channel".  This is
   core/examples/ai/SalesByRegion.e's `labelled` binding, routed through the
   shared helper instead of spelled out.

   WHAT A COMPETENT USER EXPECTS.  One call to a helper that compiles, against
   three literal tables with fully known columns.  Nothing here is polymorphic
   at the call site at all -- every row is concrete.

   WHAT ACTUALLY HAPPENS.  See this file's measurement in the family notes; the
   compile does not come back.  The identical query written WITHOUT the helper
   is Gu08, and it compiles.

   THE INCOMPLETENESS.  The definition/call-site asymmetry.  Checking `withLabel`
   against its signature (Gu06) is cheap.  Instantiating that signature is not:
   the caller gets `RUnion3`'s four constraints and `RUnion2`'s three plus the
   remainder, all sharing row variables, all at once, and that is the
   overlapping-RHS shape the solver's `commonSubexpression` rule mints fresh
   variables against.  Nothing about the USER's query is hard -- Gu08 proves it
   -- and nothing about the HELPER is hard -- Gu06 proves it.  The two together
   are.  Which means row-polymorphic abstraction, the whole point of the row
   system, is the thing that is not usable.
-}

import Prelude
import Relation
import Relation.Op as Op
import Relation.Predicate as Pred
import Syntax.Relation

field orderId, productId, channelId : Int
field units : Double
field productName, channelName, isDirect, displayName : String

orderLine = relation [
  { orderId = 5001, productId = 1, channelId = 1, units = 120.0 },
  { orderId = 5002, productId = 2, channelId = 2, units =  40.0 } ]

productDim = relation [
  { productId = 1, productName = "Desk Lamp" },
  { productId = 2, productName = "Office Chair" } ]

channelDim = relation [
  { channelId = 1, channelName = "Direct",  isDirect = "yes" },
  { channelId = 2, channelName = "Partner", isDirect = "no" } ]

sales = orderLine ** productDim ** channelDim

-- The one call the helper was written for.
labelled =
  withLabel (col_Op isDirect ==_Pred prim_Op "yes")
            (col_Op productName)
            (col_Op productName ++_Op prim_Op " via " ++_Op col_Op channelName)
            displayName
            sales

-- Same helper as Gu06, repeated here so this module stands alone.
withLabel : forall a c v pr tr er inrow outrow rel opc opa.
            ( exists o. RUnion3 v pr tr er, RUnion2 outrow inrow c,
              inrow <- (v, o), AsOp opc, AsOp opa, RelationalComb rel )
         => Predicate pr -> opc tr a -> opa er a -> Field c a -> rel inrow -> rel outrow
withLabel p whenTrue whenFalse fld = combine_Op (if_Op p whenTrue whenFalse) fld
